<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Clothes and Laundry Ideas</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For many people, clothes are the biggest source of bedroom clutter, so start there.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. A Lidded Hamper Where You Undress</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the hamper is in the bathroom but you get changed by the bed, clothes will land on the floor. Put a hamper where you actually undress, and choose one with a lid so the room still looks calm. A two-section hamper lets you sort lights and darks as you go.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Hooks for Worn-Once Clothes</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The chair in the corner usually holds clothes that are not dirty but not clean enough to put back. Give them a home instead: a short peg rail or three hooks on the wall near the closet. When the hooks are full, it is time to wash or put something away.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-04-wall-hooks.jpg" alt="Oak peg rail in a bedroom corner holding worn-once jeans, a knit sweater and a cardigan above a woven basket, next to an empty woven chair with one cushion" class="wp-image-1458" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-04-wall-hooks.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-04-wall-hooks-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-04-wall-hooks-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-04-wall-hooks-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Fold to Fit Your Drawers</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Folding clothes into small upright rectangles lets you see every T-shirt at once and fits more in each drawer. Our guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-fold-clothes-to-save-space/">how to fold clothes to save space</a> shows the file fold and a few others step by step.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Edit the Closet</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the closet is packed, clothes end up everywhere else. Take out anything that does not fit, you have not worn in a year or you keep skipping. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/closet-clean-out/">closet clean-out</a> guide walks you through it, and our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-closet-organization-ideas/">small closet organization ideas</a> help you fit more in what is left.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Surfaces and Furniture</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. One Tray on the Dresser</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A dresser top fills up fast with jewelry, bottles, receipts and cups. Put one tray on it for the things you use daily, like a watch, a ring dish and a perfume, and keep the rest of the surface clear. When the tray is full, something has to go. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/jewelry-storage-ideas/">jewelry storage ideas</a> can help with the overflow.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-03-dresser-tray.jpg" alt="Uncluttered oak dresser top with one round rattan tray holding a jewelry dish, a watch and a plain perfume bottle, next to a small potted plant and a ceramic lamp" class="wp-image-1457" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-03-dresser-tray.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-03-dresser-tray-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-03-dresser-tray-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-03-dresser-tray-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. A Three-Item Nightstand</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Limit the nightstand top to about three things, such as a lamp, a book and a glass of water. Put chargers, hand cream and earplugs in the drawer. A clear nightstand makes the whole room feel tidier.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Anchor Tall Furniture</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">While you are moving furniture around, secure dressers and tall bookcases to the wall. The U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission’s <a href="https://www.anchorit.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anchor It! campaign</a> says anchoring all furniture with drawers, doors and shelves to the wall prevents tip-over injuries and deaths. This is especially important in homes with young children.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Cube Shelves With Fabric Bins</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Cube shelves are one of the most flexible pieces of bedroom storage. Fill some cubes with matching fabric bins for small items and keep others open for books and a plant. Use one bin per category, like chargers, hobby supplies or socks, so you always know where to look.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-05-cube-shelf.jpg" alt="Oak cube storage shelf in a bedroom with sage and cream fabric bins in some cubes and books, a plant, a bowl and a woven basket in the others, under a landscape print" class="wp-image-1459" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-05-cube-shelf.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-05-cube-shelf-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-05-cube-shelf-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-05-cube-shelf-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Storage You Already Have</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. The Space Under the Bed</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Under-bed bins or rolling drawers work well for off-season clothes, spare bedding and things you use less than once a month. Choose bins with lids to keep dust out, and keep at least one side clear so you can vacuum. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/small-bedroom-storage-ideas/">small bedroom storage ideas</a> include storage beds and more.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. The Back of the Door</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An over-the-door hook rack holds a robe, a bag and tomorrow’s outfit without using any floor space. A slim over-the-door organizer can hold shoes or accessories. For more space-saving tricks, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/storage-ideas-for-small-spaces/">storage ideas for small spaces</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Shelves Above Eye Level</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Wall space above a desk or dresser is often empty. One or two shelves hold books, baskets and keepsakes and free up the surfaces below. Keep everyday items low and lighter, less-used things higher up.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Make Room for What You Love</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. A Reading or Hobby Corner</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A small corner with a chair, a lamp and a shelf gives books, yarn or sketchbooks a proper home, so they stop drifting onto the bed. Store supplies in a lidded basket or box on the shelf right next to the chair.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-06-reading-corner.jpg" alt="Bedroom reading corner with an oak bookshelf holding books, a lidded woven basket and a craft supply box, a floor lamp and a cream armchair with sage and terracotta cushions" class="wp-image-1460" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-06-reading-corner.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-06-reading-corner-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-06-reading-corner-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-06-reading-corner-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. A Clear Desk Zone</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you work or study in your bedroom, keep the desk for that job only. Give papers, chargers and stationery their own spots and clear the desk at the end of each day. Our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/desk-organization-ideas/">desk organization ideas</a> cover the details.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Keep Your Room Organized</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. A 10-Minute Daily Reset</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Once a day, spend about ten minutes putting the room back: make the bed, put clothes in the hamper or on hooks, return the dresser top to one tray, take stray items back to other rooms and clear the floor. This one habit goes a long way toward keeping a room organized.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-07-evening-reset.jpg" alt="Bedroom in the evening after a quick reset: a turned-down bed with sage and terracotta throws, a book and a glass of water on the nightstand, slippers side by side, a lidded hamper and a clear dresser in lamp light" class="wp-image-1461" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-07-evening-reset.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-07-evening-reset-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-07-evening-reset-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/organize-room-07-evening-reset-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">15. One In, One Out</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Every time something new comes into the room, like a sweater, a book or a gadget, let something similar go. Once a month, take a few minutes to refill the donate box. Our guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-declutter-your-home/">how to declutter your home</a> has more ways to keep the clutter from coming back.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mistakes to Avoid When You Organize Your Room</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Buying storage first.</strong> Sort and declutter before you buy bins, so you know what you actually need to store.</li>


<li><strong>Trying to do it all at once.</strong> Work in short timed rounds and finish one area before you start the next.</li>


<li><strong>Leaving the boxes in the room.</strong> Donations and things for other rooms should leave the same day.</li>


<li><strong>Hiding the problem.</strong> Stuffing things under the bed or in the closet without sorting just moves the mess.</li>


<li><strong>No home for worn-once clothes.</strong> Without hooks or a set spot, the chair becomes a second closet again.</li>
</ul>



